{"nodes": [{"id": "docs_design_kosmos", "label": "Kosmos", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/design.md", "source_location": "\u00a71 What Kosmos is", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "A microkernel desktop OS with a Lua userland, built to learn and to test ideas that exist but rarely appear together; it competes with nothing, which is what allows decisions a commercial OS cannot take."}, {"id": "docs_design_table_or_declared_struct", "label": "A Lua table until a declared struct", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/design.md", "source_location": "\u00a71", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "The language's data model where the shape is the caller's to choose, a declared struct where the shape is agreed. A server must stay correct when its caller is wrong, and a collector cannot sit where something else's timing depends on it. Cost: editing a header rebuilds both sides, errors become numbers, hot reload gone."}, {"id": "docs_design_principles", "label": "The seven principles", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/design.md", "source_location": "\u00a72", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Non-negotiable decisions; if a feature collides with one, the feature is cut. The 10k-line kernel figure is a symptom of 'the kernel does not know what a file is', not a rule of its own."}, {"id": "docs_design_kernel", "label": "The kernel at EL1, fixed pools, no allocator", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/design.md", "source_location": "\u00a74.1", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Everything in statically declared fixed-size pools eliminates half the possible memory bugs in one stroke. It knows nothing about files, networking, graphics or Lua."}, {"id": "docs_design_one_image", "label": "One userland image, a process is that binary with a different argument", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/design.md", "source_location": "\u00a74.1.1", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "No loader, no ELF parser, no relocation. It is what makes bootstrap possible at all: init spawns binfs, and binfs cannot be loaded from binfs."}, {"id": "docs_design_shared_image_pages", "label": "Read-only image pages mapped, not copied", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/design.md", "source_location": "\u00a74.1.1", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "2.8 MB of .text/.rodata mapped read-only at USER_TEXT_VA in every address space; only the 112 KB writable half is copied. Permissions live in the mapping, not the page, and both mappings share attributes, so the alias is safe. Took a process from 7232 KB to 2224 KB."}, {"id": "docs_design_ipc", "label": "Synchronous rendezvous IPC", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/design.md", "source_location": "\u00a74.2", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "L4 style, no kernel buffering: simpler, faster on the hot path, and it avoids the queue-and-backpressure hell that ate Mach. Destroying an endpoint must wake everyone blocked on it with an error."}, {"id": "docs_design_capabilities", "label": "Capabilities by index into a per-process array", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/design.md", "source_location": "\u00a74.3", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "A process cannot name what it was not handed: no global table to enumerate, no path to guess. Simpler than Unix permissions and better isolation."}, {"id": "docs_design_namespace", "label": "Per-process namespace", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/design.md", "source_location": "\u00a74.4", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "What a process has not mounted does not exist - not permission denied, no such path. Clients hold a namespace node rather than a permanent capability, which is what lets a server die and come back."}, {"id": "docs_design_protocol", "label": "One protocol for every resource", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/design.md", "source_location": "\u00a74.4", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Typed records rather than byte streams: list, read, write, getattr, setattr, query. read returns a table, which is where BeOS typed attributes go inside the Plan 9 primitive."}, {"id": "docs_design_coroutines", "label": "Lua coroutines as the concurrency model", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/design.md", "source_location": "\u00a74.5", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Concurrency inside a process with zero synchronisation; one server serves many clients on one stack and each conversation is written straight through with a yield where it would have waited. There are no kernel threads and no syscall to make one."}, {"id": "docs_design_net_op_poll", "label": "NET_OP_POLL and its two readiness masks", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/design.md", "source_location": "\u00a74.5", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Ready is not a single question: a reader wants to hear that bytes arrived, a writer that room appeared. The version that guessed with one mask deadlocked."}, {"id": "docs_design_smp", "label": "SMP, supported by design and off", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/design.md", "source_location": "\u00a74.5", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "With one core the bugs are deterministic; with four you debug races over UART. Known future hot spots: the page allocator, TLB shootdown and cross-core IPC."}, {"id": "docs_design_share_nothing", "label": "Share-nothing userland, one lua_State per process", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/design.md", "source_location": "\u00a72", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Concurrency complexity stays confined to the kernel."}, {"id": "docs_design_lua", "label": "Vanilla Lua 5.4", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/design.md", "source_location": "\u00a75.1", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "30k lines of readable C89, designed to be embedded in a hostile environment, first-class coroutines, and tables as the only structured type so the protocol can be the data model. LuaJIT stays out: stuck on 5.1 and hostile on bare-metal AArch64."}, {"id": "docs_design_gc", "label": "The incremental GC and its pauses", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/design.md", "source_location": "\u00a75.2", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "5.4's collector is incremental, not realtime: steps are bounded in work, not time. Mitigated by a small per-process heap, stepping by hand after the frame flip, and zero allocation in the compositing loop."}, {"id": "docs_design_sandboxing", "label": "Isolation from the hardware, not from the language", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/design.md", "source_location": "\u00a75.3", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Lua is not isolated by default and does not need to be: isolation is the EL0 address space plus capability validation. Loading precompiled bytecode is forbidden and the library list is part of the security model."}, {"id": "docs_design_no_luarocks", "label": "No LuaRocks", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/design.md", "source_location": "\u00a75.4", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Structural incompatibility rather than dogma: no dlopen, no ELF loader, no full libc, and LuaRocks is itself a Unix app. Deeper: io.open('/etc/passwd') is semantically incoherent here, so importing such libraries is POSIX through the back door."}, {"id": "docs_design_packages", "label": "Kosmos packages, resolved against the namespace", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/design.md", "source_location": "\u00a75.5", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "require resolves against the process's namespace, not a global path, so two processes can see different versions of a library without conflict."}, {"id": "docs_design_language_split", "label": "The C/Lua language split", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/design.md", "source_location": "\u00a76", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "C is what touches hardware or defines the isolation boundary; Lua is everything else. If a bug can corrupt another process it goes in C; if it can only kill its own process it goes in Lua. Almost none of the C is in the kernel - the interpreter, gfx.c and stb_truetype run at EL0."}, {"id": "docs_design_structure_or_byte_loop", "label": "Structure in Lua, loops over bytes in C", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/design.md", "source_location": "\u00a76", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Decisions, dispatch, protocol, state and layout are measurably cheap in Lua; loops over many bytes, pixels or samples are measurably expensive. Only a profile can say which."}, {"id": "docs_design_journal_checksum", "label": "The journal checksum measurement", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/design.md", "source_location": "\u00a76", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Files a second: 21.0 before the journal, 14.4 with an FNV-1a checksum in Lua, 20.5 with it stubbed out, 16.7 with it in C. The journal's own structure cost about 2%; hashing four kilobytes a byte at a time cost the other thirty."}, {"id": "docs_design_kfs_store_experiment", "label": "kfs.store as the measurement that would settle it", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/design.md", "source_location": "\u00a76", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Self-contained, already measured, and testable on the host, so a C version can be held to the same checks. Prediction on record: fifteen percent, because a file create is block I/O and round trips that no language changes."}, {"id": "docs_design_why_c_not_rust", "label": "Why C and not Rust", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/design.md", "source_location": "\u00a76", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Lua is 30k lines of C at the system's centre, so a Rust kernel gets Rust's costs with no guarantee where the risk is. And Lua's error handling uses longjmp, which skips every Drop on the hot path."}, {"id": "docs_design_c_discipline", "label": "C discipline and the mandatory flags", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/design.md", "source_location": "\u00a76", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "-std=c11 -ffreestanding -nostdlib -Wall -Wextra -Werror -fno-common -fno-strict-aliasing, plus mmio_read32/mmio_write32 with the barriers inside, stack guard pages, UBSan with an own handler, and _Static_assert on every struct crossing into assembly."}, {"id": "docs_design_no_gpu", "label": "No GPU: the framebuffer is enough", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/design.md", "source_location": "\u00a77.1", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "1920x1080 at 32bpp is 8.3 MB a frame, 500 MB/s at 60fps, against ~17 GB/s of real bandwidth - an order of magnitude spare, and with damage tracking the whole screen is never redrawn."}, {"id": "docs_design_uncached_framebuffer", "label": "The uncached framebuffer, and the backbuffer rule", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/design.md", "source_location": "\u00a77.2", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Writing to a device-mapped framebuffer is 10-50x slower than to normal RAM. Always draw into a cached backbuffer and blit one dirty rectangle at the end, which is the case write-combining handles well."}, {"id": "docs_design_fluidity", "label": "Where fluidity is won", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/design.md", "source_location": "\u00a77.3", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Input on a highest-priority thread, double buffering with damage tracking, one port and coroutine per window, vblank sync, and no blocking in the compositor. Fluidity is consistent latency, not high throughput."}, {"id": "docs_design_drawing_model", "label": "The drawing model: commands, not a shared buffer", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/design.md", "source_location": "\u00a77.4", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Model B, the X11 and BeOS app_server model: pure message passing, no new kernel primitive, and commands are data that can be logged, replayed, redirected or inspected. Shared memory is added as a special case, never the general one."}, {"id": "docs_design_drivers", "label": "Framebuffer drivers: pitch and channel order", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/design.md", "source_location": "\u00a77.5", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "The pitch is almost never width x 4 because the firmware aligns it, and the Pi's channel order is usually BGRA. Two details that cost an afternoon each."}, {"id": "docs_design_filesystem", "label": "One real filesystem on disk", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/design.md", "source_location": "\u00a78.1", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Same file for two processes, no copies and no divergent views; what does not exist is every app seeing the whole tree. It is what a container does on Linux, except here it is the only form there is."}, {"id": "docs_design_attributes_live_queries", "label": "Typed attributes and live queries", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/design.md", "source_location": "\u00a78.2", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Attributes live alongside the file, typed and indexed. A predicate registered over them makes the server push when the result set changes - the BeOS part nobody replicated."}, {"id": "docs_design_index_rebuilt_at_mount", "label": "The index is rebuilt at mount, never stored", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/design.md", "source_location": "\u00a78.3", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Derivable state that is also stored is state that can disagree with itself, which on a filesystem means a query returning a file that is not there. It holds while a mount scan is cheaper than the complexity it avoids."}, {"id": "docs_design_large_file_memory_capability", "label": "A large read returns a memory capability", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/design.md", "source_location": "\u00a78.4", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "A 936 KB PNG through fs.read gives 'not enough memory' on a 2 MB heap, so the server puts the pages in a region and the capability travels in the reply. A message is 2048 bytes, and the answer to moving a megabyte is never 'in smaller messages'."}, {"id": "docs_design_manifest", "label": "The app manifest and its declared needs", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/design.md", "source_location": "\u00a79.2", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "The launcher builds the namespace with exactly what is declared, so the app reaches nothing else because nothing else exists in its world. Even the clock is a capability."}, {"id": "docs_design_monitor_app", "label": "Monitor, the example app", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/design.md", "source_location": "\u00a79.3", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Everything it reads comes from servers exposing a namespace: /proc is not a special filesystem, it is a server answering list and read in the same protocol as the SD card."}, {"id": "docs_design_notes_app", "label": "Notes, the example app", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/design.md", "source_location": "\u00a79.4", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "A real filesystem plus attributes and live queries: fs.query registers a predicate and the server notifies when the result set changes."}, {"id": "docs_design_inspector", "label": "The inspector, the first app", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/design.md", "source_location": "\u00a79.7", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "A window listing processes and namespaces with a REPL against any live server - the tool used for the rest of development and the proof that the design closes."}, {"id": "docs_design_ui_kit", "label": "The UI kit and its consistency rule", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/design.md", "source_location": "\u00a79.6", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "An app does not draw UI primitives: the kit lives in /lib/ui and is resolved by namespace, with visual tokens in /system/ui/theme. Editing button.lua changes every app the next time one starts."}, {"id": "docs_design_hot_reload", "label": "Hot reload, removed September 2026", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/design.md", "source_location": "\u00a710", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "First demoted below responsiveness, then made moot: seven servers moved to C and there is no dynamic linking, so none can be reloaded. What survives is the shape - serve still takes a factory, so state and behaviour stay separate."}, {"id": "docs_design_supervisor_restart", "label": "Level 2: a supervisor restarts a dead server", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/design.md", "source_location": "\u00a710", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Clients reconnect through the namespace. It never depended on the language a server was written in, and it is the architectural property."}, {"id": "docs_design_state_ownership", "label": "Who owns the state when a server dies", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/design.md", "source_location": "\u00a710", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Three ways out in order of effort: state reconstructible from the client (enough for Kosmos), state in a separate store server (the good version), and persistent state (the rejected single-level store)."}, {"id": "docs_design_criticality_hierarchy", "label": "The criticality hierarchy", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/design.md", "source_location": "\u00a710", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "As in Erlang: complexity goes far from the root. The namespace is not restartable and is kept boring; framebuffer and input are restartable but visible; everything else is restartable."}, {"id": "docs_design_posix_line", "label": "The POSIX line", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/design.md", "source_location": "\u00a717", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "POSIX is four assumptions - a global tree, ambient authority, fork and signals - and all four collide with the design. A layer that works becomes a second OS sharing the kernel, and it has gravity: it is what happened to Haiku."}, {"id": "docs_design_libc", "label": "The in-process libc", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/design.md", "source_location": "\u00a717.3", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Every libc I/O function is a call into the process's namespace and nothing else, with no fallback to a global tree. Never fork, exec, signal, pipe, socket, select, ioctl or unistd.h; errno is per process."}, {"id": "docs_design_what_stays_out", "label": "What stays out, and the achievable goal", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/design.md", "source_location": "\u00a713", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "A daily driver needs a browser, and that is where projects die maintaining somebody else's port. The criterion for every decision: does this test an idea in the design, or close a feature gap against Linux?"}, {"id": "docs_design_known_risks", "label": "Known risks", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/design.md", "source_location": "\u00a714", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "No types over tens of thousands of lines of Lua, no libraries, no visual debugger, a stage that can stall for months, and the temptation to push things down to C because it is faster."}, {"id": "docs_design_diskfs", "label": "diskfs and kfs.lua", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/design.md", "source_location": "\u00a710", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "The one server left in Lua, and not for the usual reasons: its core runs on the host as well as the guest, which is what lets make test check the journal's power-loss window without booting a machine."}, {"id": "docs_design_hardware_bringup", "label": "Hardware and bring-up", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/design.md", "source_location": "\u00a711", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Development on QEMU virt, verification on real hardware. The rule for any future target: if you cannot get a character out over serial in the first two hours, it is not a target."}, {"id": "docs_design_qnx", "label": "QNX", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/design.md", "source_location": "\u00a73", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null}, {"id": "docs_design_l4", "label": "L4", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/design.md", "source_location": "\u00a73", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null}, {"id": "docs_design_plan9", "label": "Plan 9", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/design.md", "source_location": "\u00a73", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null}, {"id": "docs_design_beos", "label": "BeOS", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/design.md", "source_location": "\u00a73", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null}, {"id": "docs_design_lisp_machines", "label": "Lisp Machines", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/design.md", "source_location": "\u00a73", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null}, {"id": "docs_design_oberon", "label": "Oberon", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/design.md", "source_location": "\u00a73", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null}, {"id": "docs_design_sel4", "label": "seL4", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/design